INT. CONVENT KITCHEN - NIGHT

Whitewashed walls. A cast-iron stove, a scrubbed deal table, rows of copper pots gleaming in the lamplight. Outside, wind moves in the cypress trees.

SISTER BENEDETTA MARCHETTI-OYO (60s, black habit, flour on her sleeves) kneads dough with slow, deliberate strokes. Across the table, a novice, SISTER CLARISSA (19, wimple slightly askew), peels potatoes into a bucket. And in the doorway, a figure waits.

REVEREND MOTHER AGATHA VANDERPOOL (70s, black habit, a silver cross, a posture like a cathedral) rests a hand on the frame.

Benedetta does not look up. Clarissa's knife pauses.

AGATHA
Sister Benedetta.

BENEDETTA
Reverend Mother.

AGATHA
You are up late.

BENEDETTA
The bread will not wait.

AGATHA
Nor will the visitors.

Benedetta presses her palms into the dough. Clarissa lowers her head and peels faster.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
They arrive at dawn. The bishop's man will want to see the accounts.

BENEDETTA
They are in order.

AGATHA
Are they?

BENEDETTA
They are.

AGATHA
Then you will not mind if Sister Clarissa reads them aloud.

The knife drops. Clarissa stares at her hands. Benedetta's fingers stop moving, a long beat, then resume.

BENEDETTA
Sister Clarissa has other duties.

AGATHA
She has the best penmanship in the house.

BENEDETTA
(evenly)
She has also been awake since five.

Agatha steps into the room. The lamp throws her shadow across the table. Clarissa shrinks. Benedetta folds the dough over itself, once, twice, three times.

AGATHA
You have been with us thirty years.

BENEDETTA
Thirty-one.

AGATHA
And in all that time, I have not asked you for anything.

BENEDETTA
You have asked me for bread.

AGATHA
Bread is not asking. Bread is feeding.

Benedetta turns to the oven. Opens the iron door. A wash of heat on her face. She slides in the loaf and shuts the door.

BENEDETTA
What do you want, Mother?

AGATHA
I want to know what is missing from the ledger.

The kitchen is perfectly still. Clarissa looks up, at last, at Benedetta. Benedetta, at Agatha. The wind rises in the cypress trees.

BENEDETTA
Forty-two loaves.

AGATHA
Per week?

BENEDETTA
Per week. For thirty-one years.

AGATHA
Given to whom?

BENEDETTA
To those who knock at the back gate.

AGATHA
Without permission.

BENEDETTA
Without asking.

AGATHA
(a long pause)
I see.

She lifts the silver cross from her chest and holds it for a moment, as if weighing it. Then she lets it fall back.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
Then we will need to bake more.
